# Entry, initialisation, privileged operations and the native-code interface.

@085C0 condition_truth_table
    One 32-bit truth table per 68K condition code (T, F, HI, LS, CC, CS,
    NE, EQ, VC, VS, PL, MI, GE, LT, GT, LE).  The current N, Z, V and C
    flags are gathered into a 5-bit rotate count; rotating the table word
    by it leaves the condition's value in the low bit.  Used where a
    condition has to be evaluated without a branch (Scc, TRAPcc, and the
    trace logic's look at DBcc and Bcc).

@0C3EC set_ccr
    Load the 68K condition codes from the low five bits of data: N and Z
    into CR0, V and C into XER, X into ccr_x.  Continues through a
    replaceable handler.

@0C510 stack_pointer_slots
    Offsets in the state block of the saved stack pointer for each value of
    the SR S and M bits: USP, USP, ISP, MSP.

@0C7B0 movec_tables
    Byte tables for MOVEC: for each control register number, the offset of
    its copy in the state block (0 = not implemented), followed by offsets
    of the code that reads or writes it.

@0CA68 privilege_check_failed
    A privileged instruction was met in user state.  If the privilege
    violation vector still holds the handler address recorded in the state
    block, return and let the instruction proceed (the standard handler
    would only have emulated it); otherwise take the exception.
@0CA78 take_privilege_violation
0CA7C vector offset 0x20: privilege violation
@0CA88 require_supervisor
    Gate for privileged instructions: the dispatch table entry has loaded
    the instruction's handler from the state block into rtn_addr.  Continue
    there if in supervisor state.

@0DB94 init_state
    Fill in the emulator state block: constants, the table of replaceable
    handler addresses (copied from handler_defaults with code_ptr added),
    and the recompiler's entry points, which start out as stubs.
0DBE8 dr_code_base = cb + 0x10000
0DC0C handler_defaults - 2, for the pre-incrementing loop below
0DC14 151 entries
0DC20 offsets become addresses

@0DC44 handler_defaults
    Initial contents of the handler table at +0x800 in the state block, as
    offsets from cb (0 = no handler).

@0DE08 native_call_unavailable

@0DE1C mixed_mode_dispatch
    ===========================================================================
    Mixed mode switch
    ===========================================================================
    Entered when 68K code reaches a routine descriptor.  base_disp points
    into the descriptor (taken from the 68K stack).  Walks the descriptor's
    routine records (20 bytes each: procedure information, ISA, flags,
    procedure pointer, selector), choosing one by selector where the
    descriptor uses selectors, then either continues in 68K code at the
    procedure pointer (ISA 0) or switches to native PowerPC code (ISA 1).
    Anything else is reported to the 68K caller as an error number.
@0DE40 mixed_mode_call_native
@0DED8 mixed_mode_call_68k
0DEDC pc = procedure pointer

@0E128 switch_to_native
    Save the 68K registers where native code can find them and ask the
    kernel (trap 1) to run the native context.  Control returns here when
    native code wants the emulator again; a status byte in the native
    context block says why: 255 = resume 68K code, 240..247 = an interrupt
    arrived while native code was running, other values = an exception to
    be delivered to the 68K handler.

@0E7B4 install_exception_stubs
    Copy the second handler table (exception_stub_defaults) into the state
    block at +0x89C.
@0E7E0 exception_stub_defaults

@0E830 exit_emulator
    Store the complete 68K register state in the state block and return to
    the native code that called EmEntry.
0E870 pc of the next instruction
0E878 rebuild the full SR: system byte ...
0E87C ... X
0E884 ... N
0E888 ... Z
0E890 ... V and C
0E898 back onto the caller's stack
0E89C restore r11..r31

@0E8B0 EmEntry
    ===========================================================================
    Emulator entry point
    ===========================================================================
    Called once by native code with r3 = address of the emulator state
    block and r4 = address of the dispatch table (OpcodeTblTop).

    Saves the caller's non-volatile registers in a frame at the top of the
    state block's page, records where the code and the table are, fills in
    the emulator's context block (flags, the handler for native exceptions
    and its argument, and emulator_reset as the place to resume), and
    issues kernel trap 0, ReturnFromException.  The kernel resumes the
    context at ecb_pc, which is how emulator_reset is reached.
0E8B0 r1 = top of the 4 KB page that starts at the state block
0E8B8 not reached (the operand is 0xDEAD)

@0E8C0 EmKernelTrapTable
    Kernel service calls.  Each is an unconditional trap instruction.  The
    kernel recognises a trap taken inside this table by its address - slot
    n is service n - and resumes at the following instruction, so callers
    reach these with a branch-and-link, or with a plain branch when LR
    already holds the return address.  (It also recognises the instruction
    "twi 31, r31, n" anywhere else, by its operand.)
    .
    The service names are those of the NanoKernel disassembly in the
    powermac-rom project.  Slot 14 carries the operand 13, which is harmless
    because the slot's address decides; slot 16 is beyond the kernel's
    sixteen services.
0E8C0 0: ReturnFromException - resume this context at ecb_pc
0E8C4 1: RunAlternateContext - run native code
0E8C8 2: ResetSystem
0E8CC 3: VMDispatch
0E8D0 4: PrioritizeInterrupts
0E8D4 5: PowerDispatch
0E8D8 6: RTASDispatch
0E8DC 7: CacheDispatch
0E8E0 8: MPDispatch
0E8E4 9
0E8E8 10
0E8EC 11
0E8F0 12
0E8F4 13
0E8F8 14 (operand 13)
0E8FC 15: Thud (kernel panic)
0E900 16

@0E904 em_entry_save
0E904 save CR ...
0E90C ... LR ...
0E910 ... and r12..r31 below the top of the page
0E914 r3: the state block
0E918 r4: the dispatch table
0E920 code_ptr = this code's own address, rounded down to 64 KB
0E934 where ReturnFromException will resume
0E94C handler for native exceptions taken while emulating
0E954 its argument: where to resume for a bus error
0E960 ReturnFromException: resume at ecb_pc

@0E964 emulator_reset
    The emulator context starts here: clear the 68K registers, initialise
    the state block, then do what a 68K does on reset - load the stack
    pointer from address 0 and the pc from address 4, enter supervisor
    state with interrupts masked - and dispatch the first instruction.
0E9B4 initial SSP from the reset vector
0E9B8 initial PC
0E9C4 SR = $27xx: supervisor, interrupt mask 7
0E9D0 N = Z = 0
0E9D4 V = C = 0

@0EA00 interpreter_credits

@09660 aline_os_trap
    ===========================================================================
    Line 1010 (Mac OS trap) dispatch
    ===========================================================================
    Every line-A opcode comes to one of four routines, with addr holding
    the trap dispatcher address the
    emulator expects to find in the line-A exception vector.  If the vector
    at 40(vbr) still holds that address, the trap is dispatched here: the
    routine address is read from the trap table in 68K low memory and the
    registers are saved on the 68K stack as the standard dispatcher would
    have done.  If the vector has been changed, the exception is taken in
    the ordinary way through aline_handler.
    .
    Operating system traps (A000-A7FF) go to aline_os_trap when opcode bit
    8 is clear and aline_os_trap_alt when it is set.  Toolbox traps go to
    aline_toolbox_trap (A800-ABFF) or, with the auto-pop bit set,
    aline_toolbox_trap_autopop (AC00-AFFF).
@095E0 aline_os_trap_alt
@09720 aline_toolbox_trap
@09780 aline_toolbox_trap_autopop

@0E8C4 kcall_run_alternate_context
@0E8C8 kcall_reset_system
@0E8CC kcall_vm_dispatch
@0E8D0 kcall_prioritize_interrupts
@0E8D4 kcall_power_dispatch
@0E8DC kcall_cache_dispatch
@0E8E0 kcall_mp_dispatch
@0E8FC kcall_thud

@0CA28 stop_wait
    STOP: wait here until the kernel posts an interrupt.  Nothing in the
    loop changes the bit it tests.
